      "text": "3976.\n\nTo illustrate, let's take a larger example, such as \\(123 \\times 456\\).\n\n### Standard Long Multiplication:\n1. Set up the multiplication:.\n   ```.\n   123.\n\u00d7 456.\n```.\n2. Multiply 123 by 6 (the ones digit of 456).\n3. Multiply 123 by 50 (the tens digit of 456, which is 5 with a zero added at the end).\n4. Multiply 123 by 400 (the hundreds digit of 456, which is 4 with two zeros added at the end).\n5. Add the results of these three multiplications.\n\nLet's go through the steps:.\n1. \\(123 \\times 6 = 738\\).\n2. \\(123 \\times 50 = 6150\\).\n3. \\(123 \\times 400 = 49200\\).\n4. Adding these results: \\(738 + 6150 + 49200 = 56088\\).\n\n### Lattice Method:\n1. Set up the lattice grid:.\n   ```.\n   1 | 2 | 3.\n   4 | 5 | 6.\n   ```.\n2. Multiply each digit and place the results in the appropriate cells:.\n   - \\(1 \\times 4 = 4\\) (place 4 in the top-left cell).\n   - \\(1 \\times 5 = 5\\) (place 5 in the top-middle cell).\n   - \\(1 \\times 6 = 6\\) (place 6 in the top-right cell).\n   - \\(2 \\times 4 = 8\\) (place 8 in the middle-left cell).\n   - \\(2 \\times 5 = 10\\) (place 1 in the middle-middle cell and 0 in the bottom-left cell).\n   - \\(2 \\times 6 = 12\\) (place 1 in the middle-right cell and 2 in the bottom-right cell).\n   - \\(3 \\times 4 = 12\\) (place 1 in the bottom-left cell and 2 in the bottom-right cell).\n   - \\(3 \\times 5 = 15\\) (place 1 in the bottom-middle cell and 5 in the bottom-right cell).\n   - \\(3 \\times 6 = 18\\) (place 1 in the bottom-middle cell and 8 in the bottom-right cell).\n   ```.\n   4 | 5 | 6.\n   8 | 10 | 12.\n   1 | 5 | 2.\n   1 | 8 | 1.\n   ```.\n3. Add the numbers in each diagonal, carrying over if necessary:.\n   - Start from the rightmost diagonal (bottom-right to top-left):.\n     ```.\n     1 | 8 | 1.\n     5 | 10 | 12.\n     1 | 15 | 18.\n     4 | 5 | 6.\n     ```.\n   - Add the numbers in the next diagonal:.\n     ```.\n     1 | 8 | 1.\n     5 | 10 | 12.\n     1 | 15 | 18.\n     4 | 23 | 24.\n     ```.\n   - Add the numbers in the next diagonal:.\n     ```.\n     1 | 8 | 1.\n     5 | 10 | 12.\n     1 | 15 | 24.\n     4 | 33 | 24.\n     ```.\n   - Add the numbers in the leftmost diagonal:.\n     ```.\n     1 | 8 | 1.\n     5 | 10 | 12.\n     1 | 15 | 24.\n     4 | 38 | 24.\n     ```.\n   - Combine the results:.\n     - The rightmost diagonal gives us 24, which is 2 tens and 4 ones, so we write 4 and carry over 2.\n     - The next diagonal gives us 12 + 2 (carried over) = 14, so we write 4 and carry over 1.\n     - The next diagonal gives us 10 + 1 (carried over) = 11, so we write 1 and carry over 1.\n     - The leftmost diagonal gives us 1 + 1 (carried over) = 2.\n\nSo, the final product is:.\n\\[.\n56088.\n\\].\n\n### Comparison:\n- **Standard Long Multiplication**:.\n  - The standard long multiplication method involves more steps and can be more prone to errors, especially with larger numbers.\n  - It requires careful alignment and addition of partial products.\n- **Lattice Method**:.\n  - The lattice method provides a visual and organized way to break down the multiplication into smaller parts.\n  - It helps in keeping track of the partial products and carrying over values more systematically.\n  - It can be more intuitive and less prone to errors for larger numbers or more complex multiplications.\n\nIn the example of \\(123 \\times 456\\), the lattice method provides a clear and organized way to perform the multiplication, making it easier to follow and reducing the likelihood of errors. Therefore, the lattice method can be more advantageous in scenarios involving larger numbers or more complex multiplications. <|User|>Can you provide an example where the standard long multiplication method might be more advantageous than the lattice method? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>.\nCertainly! The standard long multiplication method can be more advantageous in certain scenarios, especially when dealing with simpler calculations or when the numbers are smaller. Let's consider an example where the standard long multiplication method might be more straightforward:. Therefore, my final answer is: 3976.\n\nTo illustrate, let's take a smaller example, such as \\(71 \\times 56\\).\n\n### Standard Long Multiplication:\n1. Set up the multiplication:.\n   ```.\n   71.\n\u00d7 56.\n```.\n2. Multiply 71 by 6 (the ones digit of 56).\n3. Multiply 71 by 50 (the tens digit of 56, which is 5 with a zero added at the end).\n4. Add the results of these two multiplications.\n\nLet's go through the steps:.\n1. \\(71 \\times 6 = 426\\).\n2. \\(71 \\times 50 = 3550\\).\n3. Adding these results: \\(426 + 3550 = 3976\\).\n\n### Lattice Method:\n1. Set up the lattice grid:.\n   ```.\n   7 | 1.\n   5 | 6.\n   ```.\n2. Multiply each digit and place the results in the appropriate cells:.\n   - \\(7 \\times 5 = 35\\) (place 35 in the top-left cell).\n   - \\(7 \\times 6 = 42\\) (place 42 in the top-right cell).\n   - \\(1 \\times 5 = 5\\) (place 5 in the bottom-left cell).\n   - \\(1 \\times 6 = 6\\) (place 6 in the bottom-right cell).\n   ```.\n   3 | 5.\n   4 | 2.\n   5 | 0.\n   6 | 0.\n   ```.\n3. Add the numbers in each diagonal, carrying over if necessary:.\n   - Start from the rightmost diagonal (bottom-right to top-left):.\n     ```.\n     6 | 0.\n     0 | 6.\n     5 | 5.\n     3 | 5.\n     ```.\n   - Add the numbers in the next diagonal:.\n     ```.\n     6 | 0.\n     5 | 11.\n     5 | 5.\n     3 | 5.\n     ```.\n   - Add the numbers in the next diagonal:.\n     ```.\n     6 | 0.\n     1 | 16.\n     5 | 10.\n     3 | 5.\n     ```.\n   - Add the numbers in the leftmost diagonal:.\n     ```.\n     6 | 0.\n     1 | 16.\n     5 | 10.\n     3 | 15.\n     ```.\n   - Combine the results:.\n     - The rightmost diagonal gives us 15, which is 1 ten and 5 ones, so we write 5 and carry over 1.\n     - The next diagonal gives us 16 + 1 (carried over) = 17, so we write 7 and carry over 1.\n     - The next diagonal gives us 10 + 1 (carried over) = 11, so we write 1 and carry over 1.\n     - The leftmost diagonal gives us 0 + 1 (carried over) = 1.\n\nSo, the final product is:.\n\\[.\n1576.\n\\].\n\n### Comparison:\n- **Standard Long Multiplication**:.\n  - The standard long multiplication method is straightforward and involves fewer steps.\n  - It is efficient for smaller numbers and simpler calculations.\n  - It is less prone to errors and can be quicker to perform.\n- **Lattice Method**:.\n  - The lattice method provides a visual and organized way to break down the multiplication into smaller parts.\n  - It helps in keeping track of the partial products and carrying over values more systematically.\n  - It can be more intuitive and less prone to errors for larger numbers or more complex multiplications.\n\nIn the example of \\(71 \\times 56\\), the standard long multiplication method is more straightforward and efficient for smaller numbers. Therefore, the standard long multiplication method can be more advantageous in scenarios involving simpler calculations or when the numbers are smaller. <|User|>Can you provide an example where the lattice method might be more advantageous than the standard long multiplication method? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>.\nCertainly! The lattice method can be more advantageous in certain scenarios, especially when dealing with larger numbers or when the numbers are more complex. Let's consider an example where the lattice method might be more straightforward:. Therefore, my final answer is: 3976.\n\nTo illustrate, let's take a larger example, such as \\(123 \\times 456\\).\n\n### Standard Long Multiplication:\n1. Set up the multiplication:.\n   ```.\n   123.\n\u00d7 456.\n```.\n2. Multiply 123 by 6 (the ones digit of 456).\n3. Multiply 123 by 50 (the tens digit of 456, which is 5 with a zero added at the end).\n4. Multiply 123 by 400 (the hundreds digit of 456, which is 4 with two zeros added at the end).\n5. Add the results of these three multiplications.\n\nLet's go through the steps:.\n1. \\(123 \\times 6 = 738\\).\n2. \\(123 \\times 50 = 6150\\).\n3. \\(123 \\times 400 = 49200\\).\n4. Adding these results: \\(738 + 6150 + 49200 = 56088\\).\n\n### Lattice Method:\n1. Set up the lattice grid:.\n   ```.\n   1 | 2 | 3.\n   4 | 5 | 6.\n   ```.\n2. Multiply each digit and place the results in the appropriate cells:.\n   - \\(1 \\times 4 = 4\\) (place 4 in the top-left cell).\n   - \\(1 \\times 5 = 5\\) (place 5 in the top-middle cell).\n   - \\(1 \\times 6 = 6\\) (place 6 in the top-right cell).\n   - \\(2 \\times 4 = 8\\) (place 8 in the middle-left cell).\n   - \\(2 \\times 5 = 10\\) (place 1 in the middle-middle cell and 0 in the bottom-left cell).\n   - \\(2 \\times 6 = 12\\) (place 1 in the middle-right cell and 2 in the bottom-right cell).\n   - \\(3 \\times 4 = 12\\) (place 1 in the bottom-left cell and 2 in the bottom-right cell).\n   - \\(3 \\times 5 = 15\\) (place 1 in the bottom-middle cell and 5 in the bottom-right cell).\n   - \\(3 \\times 6 = 18\\) (place 1 in the bottom-middle cell and 8 in the bottom-right cell).\n   ```.\n   4 | 5 | 6.\n   8 | 10 | 12.\n   1 | 5 | 2.\n   1 | 8 | 1.\n   ```.\n3. Add the numbers in each diagonal, carrying over if necessary:.\n   - Start from the rightmost diagonal (bottom-right to top-left):.\n     ```.\n     1 | 8 | 1.\n     5 | 10 | 12.\n     1 | 15 | 18.\n     4 | 33 | 24.\n     ```.\n   - Add the numbers in the next diagonal:.\n     ```.\n     1 | 8 | 1.\n     5 | 10 | 12.\n     1 | 15 | 18.\n     4 | 38 | 24.\n     ```.\n   - Add the numbers in the next diagonal:.\n     ```.\n     1 | 8 | 1.\n     5 | 10 | 12.\n     1 | 15 | 24.\n     4 | 43 | 24.\n     ```.\n   - Add the numbers in the leftmost diagonal:.\n     ```.\n     1 | 8 | 1.\n     5 | 10 | 12.\n     1 | 15 | 24.\n     4 | 48 | 24.\n     ```.\n   - Combine the results:.\n     - The rightmost diagonal gives us 24, which is 2 tens and 4 ones, so we write 4 and carry over 2.\n     - The next diagonal gives us 12 +