ddle cell and 5 in the bottom-right cell).\n   - \\(3 \\times 6 = 18\\) (place 1 in the bottom-middle cell and 8 in the bottom-right cell).\n   ```.\n   4 | 5 | 6.\n   8 | 10 | 12.\n   1 | 5 | 2.\n   1 | 8 | 1.\n   ```.\n3. Add the numbers in each diagonal, carrying over if necessary:.\n   - Start from the rightmost diagonal (bottom-right to top-left):.\n     ```.\n     1 | 8 | 1.\n     5 | 10 | 12.\n     1 | 15 | 18.\n     4 | 33 | 24.\n     ```.\n   - Add the numbers in the next diagonal:.\n     ```.\n     1 | 8 | 1.\n     5 | 10 | 12.\n     1 | 15 | 18.\n     4 | 38 | 24.\n     ```.\n   - Add the numbers in the next diagonal:.\n     ```.\n     1 | 8 | 1.\n     5 | 10 | 12.\n     1 | 15 | 24.\n     4 | 43 | 24.\n     ```.\n   - Add the numbers in the leftmost diagonal:.\n     ```.\n     1 | 8 | 1.\n     5 | 10 | 12.\n     1 | 15 | 24.\n     4 | 48 | 24.\n     ```.\n   - Combine the results:.\n     - The rightmost diagonal gives us 24, which is 2 tens and 4 ones, so we write 4 and carry over 2.\n     - The next diagonal gives us 12 + 2 (carried over) = 14, so we write 4 and carry over 1.\n     - The next diagonal gives us 10 + 1 (carried over) = 11, so we write 1 and carry over 1.\n     - The leftmost diagonal gives us 1 + 1 (carried over) = 2.\n\nSo, the final product is:.\n\\[.\n56088.\n\\].\n\n### Comparison:\n- **Standard Long Multiplication**:.\n  - The standard long multiplication method involves more steps and can be more prone to errors, especially with larger numbers.\n  - It requires careful alignment and addition of partial products.\n- **Lattice Method**:.\n  - The lattice method provides a visual and organized way to break down the multiplication into smaller parts.\n  - It helps in keeping track of the partial products and carrying over values more systematically.\n  - It can be more intuitive and less prone to errors for larger numbers or more complex multiplications.\n\nIn the example of \\(123 \\times 456\\), the lattice method provides a clear and