      "text": "2532. <|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanations and examples. Can you provide another example where the lattice method might be more advantageous than the standard algorithm? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider a slightly larger example to illustrate when the lattice method might be more advantageous than the standard algorithm. Suppose we want to multiply 123 by 456 using both methods.\n\n### Using the Standard Algorithm:\n1. \\(123 \\times 6 = 738\\)\n2. \\(123 \\times 50 = 6150\\)\n3. \\(123 \\times 400 = 49200\\)\n4. Sum the results: \\(738 + 6150 + 49200 = 56088\\)\n\n### Using the Lattice Method:\n1. **Set up the lattice grid:**\n   ```\n   1 | 2 | 3\n   - + - + -\n   4 | 5 | 6\n   ```\n\n2. **Multiply each digit and place the results in the appropriate triangles:**\n   ```\n   1 | 2 | 3\n   - + - + -\n   4 | 5 | 6\n   0 | 10 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   ```\n\n3. **Add the diagonals:**\n   ```\n   1 | 2 | 3\n   - + - + -\n   4 | 5 | 6\n   0 | 10 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 | 18\n   0 | 12 | 18\n   4 | 10 