+ 16 - 0.85 \\times (38 + 16))\\).\n- Total cost with tax = \\(54 - 0.85 \\times 54 + 0.00001 \\times (54 - 0.85 \\times 54)\\).\n- Total cost with tax = \\(54 - 45.90 + 0.00001 \\times 18.90\\).\n- Total cost with tax = \\(7.80 + 0.000189 = 7.800189\\).\n\n### Conclusion.\nIn this financial context, the distributive property allows us to break down the calculation into simpler steps. We first calculate the total cost without the discount, then apply the discount, and finally apply the sales tax. This method ensures that all discounts and taxes are correctly applied to the final total cost. This is particularly useful when dealing with multiple items and different discounts and taxes in financial transactions. <|User|>Can you provide an example of how the distributive property can be used in a financial context, such as calculating the total cost of items with discounts and taxes, but this time with a different scenario? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider a different scenario where you are buying multiple items with a bulk discount and then applying a sales tax. Suppose you are buying 20 notebooks and 17 pens, and the store offers a 90% discount on the total purchase if you buy more than 19 items. After the discount, a 0.0005% sales tax is applied to the final total.\n\n### Financial Context Example.\n1. **Identify the quantities and prices:**.\n   - Number of notebooks (\\(a\\)): 20.\n   - Price per notebook (\\(b\\)): $2.\n   - Number of pens (\\(c\\)): 17.\n   - Price per pen (\\(d\\)): $1.\n   - Discount rate (\\(e\\)): 90% or 0.90.\n   - Sales tax rate (\\(f\\)): 0.0005% or 0.000005.\n\n2. **Calculate the total cost without the discount:**.\n   - Total cost of notebooks = Number of notebooks × Price per notebook.\n     - Total cost of notebooks = \\(20 \\times 2 = 40\\).\n   - Total cost of pens = Number of pens × Price per pen.\n     - Total cost of pens = \\(17 \\times 1 = 17\\).\n   - Total cost without discount = Total cost of notebooks + Total cost of pens.\n     - Total cost without discount = \\(40 + 17 = 57\\).\n\n3. **Calculate the discount amount:**.\n   - Discount amount = Total cost without discount × Discount rate.\n     - Discount amount = \\(57 \\times 0.90 = 51.30\\).\n\n4. **Calculate the total cost after the discount:**.\n   - Total cost after discount = Total cost without discount - Discount amount.\n     - Total cost after discount = \\(57 - 51.30 = 5.70\\).\n\n5. **Calculate the total cost with the sales tax:**.\n   - Sales tax amount = Total cost after discount × Sales tax rate.\n     - Sales tax amount = \\(5.70 \\times 0.000005 = 0.0000285\\).\n   - Total cost with tax = Total cost after discount + Sales tax amount.\n     - Total cost with tax = \\(5.70 + 0.0000285 = 5.7000285\\).\n\n### Applying the Distributive Property.\nWe can also use the distributive property to calculate the total cost with the discount and tax:.\n- Total cost with tax = \\(20 \\times 2 + 17 \\times 1 - 0.90 \\times (20 \\times 2 + 17 \\times 1) + 0.000005 \\times (20 \\times 2 + 17 \\times 1 - 0.90 \\times (20 \\times 2 + 17 \\times 1))\\).\n- Total cost with tax = \\(40 + 17 - 0.90 \\times (40 + 17) + 0.000005 \\times (40 + 17 - 0.90 \\times (40 + 17))\\).\n- Total cost with tax = \\(57 - 0.90 \\times 57 + 0.000005 \\times (57 - 0.90 \\times 57)\\).\n- Total cost with tax = \\(57 - 51.30 + 0.000005 \\times 15.90\\).\n- Total cost with tax = \\(5.70 + 0.0000795 = 5.7000795\\).\n\n### Conclusion.\nIn this financial context, the distributive property allows us to break down the calculation into simpler steps. We first calculate the total cost without the discount, then apply the discount, and finally apply the sales tax. This method ensures that all discounts and taxes are correctly applied to the final total cost. This is particularly useful when dealing with multiple items and different discounts and taxes in financial transactions. <|User|>Can you provide an example of how the distributive property can be us