odel for multiplication:.\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values**:.\n   - \\(85\\) can be broken down into \\(80 + 5\\).\n   - \\(73\\) can be broken down into \\(70 + 3\\).\n\n2. **Visualize the rectangle**:.\n   - Draw a rectangle and divide it into four smaller rectangles based on the place values.\n   - The dimensions of the large rectangle will be \\(80 + 5\\) by \\(70 + 3\\).\n\n3. **Calculate the area of each smaller rectangle**:.\n   - \\(80 \\times 70 = 5600\\).\n   - \\(80 \\times 3 = 240\\).\n   - \\(5 \\times 70 = 350\\).\n   - \\(5 \\times 3 = 15\\).\n\n4. **Sum the areas of the smaller rectangles**:.\n   - Add all the areas together:.\n     \\[.\n     5600 + 240 + 350 + 15 = 6205.\n     \\].\n\nSo, using the area model, the product of 85 and 73 is 6205. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the standard algorithm for multiplication, but this time with a different number, say 98 and 56? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(98 \\times 56\\) using the standard algorithm:.\n\n1. **Write the numbers in a vertical format**:.\n   ```.\n   98.\n× 56.\n```.\n\n2. **Multiply 98 by the units digit of 56 (which is 6)**:.\n   - First, multiply 98 by 6:.\n     ```.\n     98.\n   ×  6.\n   -----.\n     588.\n     ```.\n   - Write 588 below the line, aligning the digits by place value.\n\n3. **Multiply 98 by the tens digit of 56 (which is 5), and remember to shift one place to the left**:.\n   - First, multiply 98 by 5:.\n     ```.\n     98.\n   ×  5.\n   -----.\n     490.\n     ```.\n   - Since we are multiplying by the tens place, we shift the result one place to the left:.\n     ```.\n     98.\n   ×  5.\n   -----.\n     4900.\n     ```.\n\n4. **Add the two results together**:.\n   - Write the results of the two multiplications one below the other and add them:.\n     ```.\n     98.\n   ×  56.\n   -----.\n     588.\n   +4900.\n   -----.\n     5488.\n     ```.\n\n5. **The final product is**:.\n   ```.\n   98.\n   × 56.\n   -----.\n   5488.\n   ```.\n\nSo, using the standard algorithm, the product of 98 and 56 is 5488. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the partial products method? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(98 \\times 56\\) using the partial products method:.\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values**:.\n   - \\(98\\) can be broken down into \\(90 + 8\\).\n   - \\(56\\) can be broken down into \\(50 + 6\\).\n\n2. **Multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number**:.\n   - \\(90 \\times 50\\).\n   - \\(90 \\times 6\\).\n   - \\(8 \\times 50\\).\n   - \\(8 \\times 6\\).\n\n3. **Calculate each partial product**:.\n   - \\(90 \\times 50 = 4500\\).\n   - \\(90 \\times 6 = 540\\).\n   - \\(8 \\times 50 = 400\\).\n   - \\(8 \\times 6 = 48\\).\n\n4. **Sum the partial products**:.\n   - Add all the partial products together:.\n     \\[.\n     4500 + 540 + 400 + 48 = 5488.\n     \\].\n\nSo, using the partial products method, the product of 98 and 56 is 5488. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the grid method? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(98 \\times 56\\) using the grid method:.\n\n1. **Set up the grid**:.\n   - Write the digits of 98 along the top of the grid and the digits of 56 along the left side of the grid.\n   ```.\n   9 | 8.\n   ───┼───.\n   5 | 6.\n   ```.\n\n2. **Multiply each digit in the top row by each digit in the left column**:.\n   - Multiply 9 by 5, 9 by 6, 8 by 5, and 8 by 6.\n   ```.\n   9 | 8.\n   ───┼───.\n   5 | 6.\n   9 | 48.\n   8 | 40.\n   8 | 48.\n   ```.\n\n3. **Write the results in the grid**:.\n   - Place the products in the corresponding cells of the grid.\n   ```.\n   9 | 8.\n   ───┼───.\n   5 | 6.\n   9 | 48.\n   8 | 40.\n   8 | 48.\n   ```.\n\n4. **Add the numbers in each diagonal**:.\n   - Start from the bottom left and move diagonally to th