del, the product of 72 and 34 is 2448. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the standard algorithm for multiplication, but this time with a different number, say 89 and 45? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(89 \\times 45\\) using the standard algorithm:.\n\n1. **Write the numbers in a vertical format**:.\n   ```.\n   89.\n× 45.\n```.\n\n2. **Multiply 89 by the units digit of 45 (which is 5)**:.\n   - First, multiply 89 by 5:.\n     ```.\n     89.\n   ×  5.\n   -----.\n     445.\n     ```.\n   - Write 445 below the line, aligning the digits by place value.\n\n3. **Multiply 89 by the tens digit of 45 (which is 4), and remember to shift one place to the left**:.\n   - First, multiply 89 by 4:.\n     ```.\n     89.\n   ×  4.\n   -----.\n     356.\n     ```.\n   - Since we are multiplying by the tens place, we shift the result one place to the left:.\n     ```.\n     89.\n   ×  4.\n   -----.\n     3560.\n     ```.\n\n4. **Add the two results together**:.\n   - Write the results of the two multiplications one below the other and add them:.\n     ```.\n     89.\n   ×  45.\n   -----.\n     445.\n   +3560.\n   -----.\n     4005.\n     ```.\n\n5. **The final product is**:.\n   ```.\n   89.\n   × 45.\n   -----.\n   4005.\n   ```.\n\nSo, using the standard algorithm, the product of 89 and 45 is 4005. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the partial products method? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(89 \\times 45\\) using the partial products method:.\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values**:.\n   - \\(89\\) can be broken down into \\(80 + 9\\).\n   - \\(45\\) can be broken down into \\(40 + 5\\).\n\n2. **Multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number**:.\n   - \\(80 \\times 40\\).\n   - \\(80 \\times 5\\).\n   - \\(9 \\times 40\\).\n   - \\(9 \\times 5\\).\n\n3. **Calculate each partial product**:.\n   - \\(80 \\times 40 = 3200\\).\n   - \\(80 \\times 5 = 400\\).\n   - \\(9 \\times 40 = 360\\).\n   - \\(9 \\times 5 = 45\\).\n\n4. **Sum the partial products**:.\n   - Add all the partial products together:.\n     \\[.\n     3200 + 400 + 360 + 45 = 4005.\n     \\].\n\nSo, using the partial products method, the product of 89 and 45 is 4005. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the grid method? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(89 \\times 45\\) using the grid method:.\n\n1. **Set up the grid**:.\n   - Write the digits of 89 along the top of the grid and the digits of 45 along the left side of the grid.\n   ```.\n   8 | 9.\n   ───┼───.\n   4 | 5.\n   ```.\n\n2. **Multiply each digit in the top row by each digit in the left column**:.\n   - Multiply 8 by 4, 8 by 5, 9 by 4, and 9 by 5.\n   ```.\n   8 | 9.\n   ───┼───.\n   4 | 5.\n   8 | 40.\n   9 | 36.\n   9 | 45.\n   ```.\n\n3. **Write the results in the grid**:.\n   - Place the products in the corresponding cells of the grid.\n   ```.\n   8 | 9.\n   ───┼───.\n   4 | 5.\n   8 | 40.\n   9 | 36.\n   9 | 45.\n   ```.\n\n4. **Add the numbers in each diagonal**:.\n   - Start from the bottom left and move diagonally to the top right, adding the numbers in each diagonal.\n   - If the sum is a two-digit number, carry over the tens digit to the next diagonal.\n   ```.\n   8 | 9.\n   ───┼───.\n   4 | 5.\n   8 | 40.\n   9 | 36.\n   9 | 45.\n   ───┼───.\n   360 | 405.\n   ```.\n\n5. **Read the final product from the top left to the bottom right**:.\n   - The final product is 4005.\n\nSo, using the grid method, the product of 89 and 45 is 4005. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the area model for multiplication? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(89 \\times 45\\) using the area model for multiplication:.\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values**:.\n   - \\(89\\) can be broken down into \\(80 + 9\\).\n   - \\(45\\) can be broken down into \\(40 + 5\\).\n\n2. **Visualize the rectangle**:.\n   - Draw a rectangle and divide it into four smaller rectangles based on the place values.\n   - The dimensions of the large rectangle will be \\(80 + 9\\) by \\(40 + 5\\).\n\n3. **Calculate the area of each smaller rectangle**:.\n   - \\(80 \\times 40 = 3200\\).\n   - \\(80 \\times 5 = 400\\).\n   - \\(9 \\times 40 = 360\\).\n   - \\(9 \\times 5 = 45\\).\n\n4. **Sum the areas of the smaller rectangles**:.\n   - Add all the areas together:.\n     \\[.\n     3200 + 400 + 360 + 45 = 4005.\n     \\].\n\nSo, using the area model, the product of 89 and 45 is 4005. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the standard algorithm for multiplication, but this time with a different number, say 67 and 54? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(67 \\times 54\\) using the standard algorithm:.\n\n1. **Write the numbers in a vertical format**:.\n   ```.\n   67.\n× 54.\n```.\n\n2. **Multiply 67 by the units digit of 54 (which is 4)**:.\n   - First, multiply 67 by 4:.\n     ```.\n     67.\n   ×  4.\n   -----.\n     268.\n     ```.\n   - Write 268 below the line, aligning the digits by place value.\n\n3. **Multiply 67 by the tens digit of 54 (which is 5), and remember to shift one place to the left**:.\n   - First, multiply 67 by 5:.\n     ```.\n     67.\n   ×  5.\n   -----.\n     335.\n     ```.\n   - Since we are multiplying by the tens place, we shift the result one place to the left:.\n     ```.\n     67.\n   ×  5.\n   -----.\n     3350.\n     ```.\n\n4. **Add the two results together**:.\n   - Write the results of the two multiplications one below the other and add them:.\n     ```.\n     67.\n   ×  54.\n   -----.\n     268.\n   +3350.\n   -----.\n     3618.\n     ```.\n\n5. **The final product is**:.\n   ```.\n   67.\n   × 54.\n   -----.\n   3618.\n   ```.\n\nSo, using the standard algorithm, the product of 67 and 54 is 3618. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the partial products method? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(67 \\times 54\\) using the partial products method:.\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values**:.\n   - \\(67\\) can be broken down into \\(60 + 7\\).\n   - \\(54\\) can be broken down into \\(50 + 4\\).\n\n2. **Multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number**:.\n   - \\(60 \\times 50\\).\n   - \\(60 \\times 4\\).\n   - \\(7 \\times 50\\).\n   - \\(7 \\times 4\\).\n\n3. **Calculate each partial product**:.\n   - \\(60 \\times 50 = 3000\\).\n   - \\(60 \\times 4 = 240\\).\n   - \\(7 \\times 50 = 350\\).\n   - \\(7 \\times 4 = 28\\).\n\n4. **Sum the partial products**:.\n   - Add all the partial products together:.\n     \\[.\n     3000 + 240 + 350 + 28 = 3618.\n     \\].\n\nSo, using the partial products method, the product of 67 and 54 is 3618. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the grid method? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem \\(67 \\times 54\\) using the grid method:.\n\n1. **Set up the grid**:.\n   - Write the digits of 67 along the top of the grid and the digits of 54 along the left side of the grid.\n   ```.\n   6 | 7.\n   ───┼───.\n   5 | 4.\n   ```.\n\n2. **Multiply each digit in the top row by each digit in the left column**:.\n   - Multiply 6 by 5, 6 by 4, 7 by 5, and 7 by 4.\n   ```.\n   6 | 7.\n   ───┼───.\n   5 | 4.\n   6 | 28.\n   7 | 35.\n   7 | 28.\n   ```.\n\n3. **Write the results in the grid**:.\n   - Place the products in the corresponding cells of the grid.\n   ```.\n   6 | 7.\n   ───┼───.\n   5 | 4.\n   6 | 28.\n   7 | 35.\n   7 | 28.\n   ```.\n\n4. **Add the numbers in each diagonal**:.\n   - Start from the bottom left and move diagonally to the top right, adding the numbers in each diagonal.\n   - If the sum is a two-digit number, carry over the tens digit to the ne